{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BVXEpHH8W7NmPxyb4tmpBr","type":"tool_result","content":"CONTENTS.md\nLICENSE\nREADME.md\nhistory.md\nlean\noverview.pdf\noverview.tex\npreprints\nreasoning_traces\n                                 Apache License\n                           Version 2.0, January 2004\n                        http://www.apache.org/licenses/\n\n   TERMS AND CONDITIONS FOR USE, REPRODUCTION, AND DISTRIBUTION\nComparatorChallenges\nLICENSE\nOAI\nOAI.lean\nREADME.md\ndocs\nformalization.yaml\nlake-manifest.json\nlakefile.lean\nlean-toolchain\n# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n\n## Scope\n\nFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n\nFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n\n## Comparator links\n\n| Result | Comparator statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n----\nleanprover--lean4---v4.29.0\nleanprover--lean4---v4.34.0-rc2\nCli\nLeanSearchClient\nQq\naesop\nbatteries\nimportGraph\nmathlib\nplausible\nproofwidgets","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0ef12acb-f9ad-4899-992d-6d80c628ee09","uuid":"250ada7c-7991-466b-ae9f-20906e9c0bab","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:43:04.166Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"CONTENTS.md\nLICENSE\nREADME.md\nhistory.md\nlean\noverview.pdf\noverview.tex\npreprints\nreasoning_traces\n                                 Apache License\n                           Version 2.0, January 2004\n                        http://www.apache.org/licenses/\n\n   TERMS AND CONDITIONS FOR USE, REPRODUCTION, AND DISTRIBUTION\nComparatorChallenges\nLICENSE\nOAI\nOAI.lean\nREADME.md\ndocs\nformalization.yaml\nlake-manifest.json\nlakefile.lean\nlean-toolchain\n# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n\n## Scope\n\nFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n\nFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n\n## Comparator links\n\n| Result | Comparator statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n----\nleanprover--lean4---v4.29.0\nleanprover--lean4---v4.34.0-rc2\nCli\nLeanSearchClient\nQq\naesop\nbatteries\nimportGraph\nmathlib\nplausible\nproofwidgets","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BVXEpHH8W7NmPxyb4tmpBr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
