{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QkWMTcSVKNnKXayjyiSQ9s","type":"tool_result","content":"# loop0014 item 02 — the formal frame (2026-11-04)\n\nLean item: `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean`\n(verbatim copy of OpenAI's machine model and CNF encoding, Apache-2.0) and\n`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` (ours). The report\nsection is `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §2. No reduction or encoding of\npathwidth is stated in this item, so TASK.md's brute-force check to 7\nvertices has nothing to check; the two checks that apply are below.\n\n- `axiom_check.lean`, `axiom_check.log` — `#print axioms` of every\n  declaration of `Frame.lean`: all on `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`.\n- `body_diff.log` — the body of `SubexpCount` diffed line by line against\n  lines 195–217 of the comparator theorem `exact_count_and_sat`\n  (leading whitespace and the theorem's trailing `:= by` normalised):\n  identical, 23 lines.\n- The verbatim copy itself was verified byte for byte when it was made\n  (`diff` of comparator lines 1–190 and 220 against the copy after its\n  header: empty); regenerate as below.\n\nRegenerate:\n\n    cd ~/dev/MOSP\n    F=~/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean\n    sha256sum $F                      # 4183b205…76685\n    diff <(sed -n 1,190p $F; sed -n 220p $F) \\\n         <(sed -n '/^Everything after this comment block is the copy/,$p' lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean | tail -n +3)\n    diff <(sed -n 195,217p $F | sed 's/^ *//; s/ := by$//') \\\n         <(sed -n '/^def SubexpCount/,/^def ExactCountAndSat/p' lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean | sed -n '2,24p' | sed 's/^ *//')\n    (cd lean && lake build && lake env lean ../reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/02_frame/axiom_check.lean)\n    python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0014/gate.py\n---\nThe item asked whether to copy OpenAI's `PrimitiveMachine` model or to use\nan abstract \"decidable predicate with a time function\". **Copied, verbatim,\ninto its own file.** `PrimitiveMachine.lean` is lines 1–190 and 220 of\n`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` in the clone\n(commit `4eaee875`, 2026-10-29; file SHA-256 `4183b205…76685`), i.e. every\ndefinition of the comparator file — the CNF syntax and `count`, the\ncanonical encoding `encodeCNF` and `formulaBitLength`, `PrimitiveMachine`\n(a multi-tape machine over Mathlib's `Turing.TM0.Stmt` and `Turing.Tape`,\none scanned tape per state, halting into a `Bool`), `Trace`, the located\nconfiguration that monitors visited cells, `runtimeScale` and\n`runtimeBound` — with the namespaces unchanged, under an Apache-2.0 header\nnaming the origin, and **without** lines 191–219, the theorem\n`exact_count_and_sat` and its `sorry`. The copy was checked byte for byte\nwhen made (`02_frame/README.md`); it compiles unchanged on our Mathlib\n(`1192d624`, toolchain `v4.34.0-rc2` against their `v4.34.1`). The clone\nitself was read with `cat` only and nothing in it was built or run.\n\nWhy copy and not abstract:\n\n1. **Fidelity.** With the model copied, `SubexpCount k` is the comparator's\n   statement *character for character* (`02_frame/body_diff.log`: the 23\n   lines of the body are identical after stripping indentation and the\n   theorem's `:= by`). An abstract notion would be our translation of the\n   theorem into another model, and the translation — \"their machine is\n   polynomially equivalent to this notion\" — would be one more thing to\n   trust, with nothing in Lean to check it against.\n2. **Non-vacuity.** Without a cost model, \"`∃ f` computing the count and\n   `∃ T` subexponential with `time ≤ T`\" is provable outright (take the\n   mathematical `count` and any `T`): the hypothesis would be a theorem and\n   every conditional result empty. An abstract notion that is not vacuous\n   must carry its own cost axioms — more assumptions, not fewer.\n---\nnamespace MOSPFormalization\n\nvariable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\nvariable (G : SimpleGraph V)\n\n/-- The pathwidth of a graph: minimum width over all path decompositions. -/\nnoncomputable def pathwidth : ℕ :=\n  sInf (Set.range (fun P : PathDecomposition G => P.width))\n\n/-- Pathwidth is at most the width of any specific decomposition. -/\ntheorem pathwidth_le_width (P : PathDecomposition G) :\n    pathwidth G ≤ P.width := by\n  apply Nat.sInf_le\n  exact ⟨P, rfl⟩\n\n/-- Pathwidth is at most `Fintype.card V - 1` (trivial decomposition). -/\n---\n29:\"no `2^{o(n)}` algorithm unless ETH fails\" statement in the width\n37:but not `2^{o(n)}`. The ETH paper's Corollary 1.3 gives `2^{o(v)}` for\n44:> **Q.** Does pathwidth admit a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm on `n`-vertex\n46:> theorem? And if not: which surviving hypothesis would a `2^{o(n)}`\n63:  and the search is near its floor. A `2^{o(n)}` algorithm must not\n87:   `2^{o(n)}`; a quadratic one transfers nothing. Every reduction in the\n114:| 03 | The reduction catalogue: every linear-in-vertices reduction *into* pathwidth / vertex separation on record, and the best `X` such that `PathwidthSubexp → X ∈ 2^{o(N)}`; each candidate checked on all graphs to 7 vertices | 01 | reading, code | 4 h |\n117:| 06 | Measurement: `|F_k|` and `|R_f|` at `k = pw` against `n` on random cubic graphs and `G(n, 3/n)` to `n = 60` (`learning/two_ended.py`); the exponential rate; is the reachable lattice ever `2^{o(n)}` on a class where pw is NP-hard? | — | compute (minutes) | 2 h |\n118:| 07 | The reduction theorem, on paper: `PathwidthSubexp → X ∈ 2^{o(N)}` for item 03's `X`, with the reduction's correctness proved; if item 03 finds only quadratic reductions, the theorem is \"no linear reduction into pathwidth is on record\" and item 07 tries to build one from Hamiltonian path or bandwidth | 03 | mathematics | 4 h |\n120:| 09 | The positive attempt: if item 04 found a class with `O(n)`-bit layouts and bounded-width constraints, write the encoding and prove `SubexpCount k → pathwidth on the class ∈ 2^{o(n)}`; else the attempt on the chain count (item 05's route) with a stated kill | 04, 05 | mathematics, Lean | 4 h |\n121:| 10 | The hypothesis map: which published lower bounds on width parameters rested on ETH or SETH (with citations and the exact statement voided), and which hypotheses survive (`P ≠ NP`, `NP ⊄ SUBEXP` in input length, the Set Cover and Orthogonal Vectors conjectures' status); a table for the note | 01 | reading, writing | 3 h |\n135:- **Item 01 kills the loop** if a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm for\n184:instance. It claims, at the end, one of: a conditional `2^{o(n)}` algorithm\n186:reduction theorem naming what a `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm would refute","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JBZzBGJ8PbK3yQ8iGYoZD6","agent_id":"a8af74a5bb59c2862","session_id":"0ef12acb-f9ad-4899-992d-6d80c628ee09","uuid":"c3d25560-9658-4810-bfab-78dbc679b8bd","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:54:49.030Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QkWMTcSVKNnKXayjyiSQ9s","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter read of Frame.lean"}
